  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Divers found the crew still seated at their stations. Eight men. Inside a Confederate submarine that had been missing on the seabed since 1864.",
        "visual_query": "charleston harbor south carolina coastline",
        "visual_fallback": "murky ocean floor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "February 1864, off Charleston. The Hunley drove a spar torpedo into the USS Housatonic, sinking it in three minutes. History's first submarine kill.",
        "visual_query": "civil war era wooden warship",
        "visual_fallback": "old sailing ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then it never came home. Twenty-one men died inside this boat across three separate sinkings. So what killed the final eight?",
        "visual_query": "shipwreck diver atlantic coast water",
        "visual_fallback": "deep underwater wreck"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Located in 1995, raised in 2000. No skeleton showed injury. The pump was never set to clear water. Nobody had released the escape weights.",
        "visual_query": "charleston conservation laboratory submarine hull",
        "visual_fallback": "rusted metal hull"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2017 Duke researchers named the likely killer: their own torpedo. A hundred and thirty-five pounds of powder, sixteen feet away. The blast wave went through the hull, and through them.",
        "visual_query": "underwater explosion shockwave dark sea",
        "visual_fallback": "dark rolling water"
      }
    ],
